Modifications relative to upstream HyperDNS
-------------------------------------------

DigitDNS is a rebranded and extended fork. In accordance with section 5 of the
GNU AGPL-3.0, the following changes were made to the upstream work:

  * The browser-facing web interface (landing page, login page, dashboard,
    subscriber portal, i18n strings, error pages and subscription defaults) is
    rebranded from "HyperDNS" to "DigitDNS". This is the primary modification.
  * The dashboard section formerly titled "Custom Proxied Domains" is renamed
    to "Proxied Domains".
  * The Proxied Domains list gained sorting controls and incremental
    (infinite-scroll) loading.
  * The Live Query Log view was made full-height and each row gained a quick
    action menu (Proxy / Block / Direct / Remove).
  * The upstream resolver ("racer") gained DoQ (`quic://` / `doq://`, RFC 9250),
    DNS-over-HTTP/3 (`h3://` / `doh3://`) and DNSCrypt (`sdns://` DNS stamps)
    transports alongside the upstream UDP, TCP, DoT and DoH ones. This adds the
    third-party dependencies `github.com/quic-go/quic-go`,
    `github.com/ameshkov/dnscrypt/v2` and `github.com/ameshkov/dnsstamps`.
  * The in-dashboard self-updater is repointed from the upstream release
    channel to this fork's release channel and re-signed with this fork's own
    pinned ed25519 release key.
  * This fork does not track or merge upstream: the `upstream-sync` GitHub
    Actions workflow that once merged upstream changes into the `custom`
    branch was removed, and no automatic sync is performed.

Internal identifiers were renamed with the rest of the rebrand: the
`X-HyperDNS-*` HTTP headers became `X-DigitDNS-*`, the systemd unit and
service became `digitdns`, the Go module path is `digitdns`, the certificate
subject uses `digitdns.local`, and the interactive terminal command is
`digitdns` (the `hdns` name is retired).

Two classes of identifier are deliberately left unchanged, because renaming
them would break existing installs rather than rebrand them:

  * Storage keys. The persisted API-key stamp keeps its legacy `hdns_live_`
    prefix in stored data; an existing `hdns_live_` API key is rotated in
    place to `digitdns_live_` at first start of the upgraded binary, with the
    secret preserved, so callers of the REST API keep working. Dashboard
    login sessions are held in memory only, so the restart that the upgrade
    already requires ends them and the operator simply logs in once more; no
    session data is stored or migrated. The blind-index HKDF label
    (`hyperdns:blind-index:v1`) and the weak-password dictionary entry
    `hyperdns` must never change: the first would silently invalidate every
    operator's stored blind indexes, and the second would silently stop
    flagging that password.

  * Local browser state. The dashboard keeps its existing `window.__hdns`
    bridge name and the `hyperdns_token` / `hyperdns_theme` localStorage
    keys so that a browser already logged into the panel stays logged in
    across the upgrade. These are never rendered to the operator.

